<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herat is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, historically a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>, especially in the fifteenth century, it became a capital and a leading centre of Persian art, literature and scholarship. It was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat-school">Herat school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herat.jpg" alt="Herat" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yves Picq · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herat_7452a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Timur marched from <b>Herat</b>, Shah Wali and his sons were assassinated at Farah, Sulaiman surrendered, and Timur took the throne in November 1772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ghor, a remote and mountainous region of central Afghanistan between <b>Herat</b> and Ghazni, was among the last parts of the eastern Iranian world to accept Islam, and its people lived in fortified villages under local chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They may have built fortified grid-plan cities at Bukhara, Panjikent and <b>Herat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the east, Qajar attempts to recover <b>Herat</b>, once part of the Safavid realm, failed: Mohammad Shah besieged it in vain in 1837–1838, and when Naser al-Din Shah took it in 1856 Britain declared war and forced Iran to renounce its claims in the Treaty of Paris of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The cities kept their crafts: Tabriz was a centre of artists and calligraphers, a group of whom Baysunghur carried off to <b>Herat</b> in 1421.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This reformed army enabled Abbas to recover <b>Herat</b> and Khorasan from the Uzbeks in 1598, retake Tabriz and Azerbaijan from the Ottomans between 1603 and 1607, expel the Portuguese from Hormuz with English naval help in 1622, and seize Baghdad and most of Iraq in 1623–1624, a sequence…</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
